 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "sive-tricolon",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Tricolon",
   "quote": "sive perfidia ... sive invidia ... sive quod ... gestiebat",
   "trans": "whether through treachery ... or envy ... or because it longed ...",
   "body": "The narrator offers three guesses for why the lamp spilled its oil, in a rising tricolon of <em>sive</em> clauses: wicked treachery, harmful envy, or simple desire to touch the god. The mock-uncertainty teases the reader and personifies the lamp as a jealous accomplice.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The tricolon <em>sive ... sive ... sive</em> playfully speculates on the lamp’s motive, personifying it as treacherous, envious or love-struck — a witty digression that delays and heightens the catastrophe.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "apostrophe",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Apostrophe",
   "quote": "hem audax et temeraria lucerna",
   "trans": "Ah, bold and rash lamp!",
   "body": "The narrator breaks off to address the lamp directly (<em>apostrophe</em>), scolding it as <em>audax</em> and <em>temeraria</em> (\"bold and rash\"). The sudden second person and the exclamation <em>hem</em> bring mock-epic indignation to the little household object.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The apostrophe to the <em>lucerna</em> (<em>hem audax et temeraria lucerna</em>) injects mock-heroic outrage: the narrator rounds on a mere lamp as if on a villain, the direct address heightening the drama of the betrayal.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "vile-ministerium",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "amoris vile ministerium",
   "trans": "base servant of love",
   "body": "The lamp is named <em>amoris vile ministerium</em> — \"love’s cheap/base servant\". The object that should serve love has instead betrayed it; the contemptuous <em>vile</em> turns the helpful lamp into a treacherous menial.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"Calling the lamp <em>amoris vile ministerium</em> is pointedly ironic: the humble servant of the lovers’ night has become the agent of their ruin, <em>vile</em> dripping with the narrator’s scorn.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "ignis-deum",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Paradox",
   "quote": "ipsum ignis totius deum aduris",
   "trans": "you are scorching the very god of all fire",
   "body": "A neat paradox: a tiny lamp-flame <em>aduris</em> (\"you scorch\") the <em>ignis totius deus</em>, the god who rules all fire — Love, whose flames burn gods and men. The little flame burns the lord of all flame.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The paradox <em>ignis totius deum aduris</em> sharpens the outrage: a household lamp injures the very god of all fire, Love himself — the small flame wounding the source of all burning passion.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "tacitus-avolavit",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Pathos",
   "quote": "infelicissimae coniugis tacitus avolavit",
   "trans": "in silence he flew away from his most ill-fated wife",
   "body": "The chapter ends on quiet desolation: the god leaves <em>tacitus</em> (\"in silence\"), and Psyche is <em>infelicissima coniunx</em> (\"his most ill-fated wife\"). No reproach, no farewell — just silence, the superlative loading the loss with pathos.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The closing <em>tacitus avolavit</em> and the superlative <em>infelicissimae coniugis</em> end the scene in bleak pathos: Cupid’s silent departure and Psyche’s ‘most ill-fated’ status seal the catastrophe wordlessly.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
